\chapter{Network route and identity}
\label{ch:network}

Every request scholar makes leaves the machine by some route and under
some name.
Both used to be accidental.
The route was whatever [[requests]] made of the environment: it honours
[[HTTPS_PROXY]], so a user who needed a campus address---for a
subscription that a publisher grants by IP, or for the Scopus view that
Elsevier serves only to the subscribing network (\cref{sec:scopus-view})
---could route scholar through an SSH tunnel by exporting that variable.
But nothing documented it, nothing reported whether it had taken effect,
and a typo in the variable name fell back to the direct route without a
word.
The name was [[python-requests/2.x]], the library's default, except
where a provider had added its own; and publishers turn that name away.
Through a working campus tunnel, Springer refused [[python-requests]]
while serving the same PDF to [[curl]].

This chapter makes both settings explicit, in one module that every
outgoing request consults:
\begin{description}
\item[The route] A proxy given by the global [[--proxy URL]] option or the
  [[SCHOLAR_PROXY]] variable, applied to every request; when neither is
  set, [[requests]]' own handling of [[HTTP(S)_PROXY]] applies as before.
  [[scholar providers check]] states the route, and can ask a third party
  for the exit address.
\item[The identity] A User-Agent that names scholar, its version and
  its home page: [[scholar/1.27 (+https://github.com/dbosk/scholar)]].
  [[SCHOLAR_USER_AGENT]] overrides it.
\end{description}

What the identity is \emph{not} is a disguise.
A browser-like User-Agent would not even help: in the same experiment,
Springer answered a browser string with a \enquote{Client Challenge}
page, and the large publishers (Elsevier, Wiley, Taylor~\&~Francis,
ACM, \dots) put every script behind Cloudflare or a login page whatever
it calls itself.
Those checks are the publisher saying no.
Scholar treats them as a stopping point---the PDF is not available to
this client---and does not try to get around them, neither here nor in
the PDF module (\cref{pdf-module}).
An honest name is what lets the services that do welcome tools
(OpenAlex's and Crossref's polite pools, the small publishers and
repositories) tell scholar apart from abuse.


\section{Design overview}

The module has three parts:
\begin{description}
\item[Settings] [[proxy_url()]] and [[user_agent()]] answer \enquote{which
  route?} and \enquote{which name?} from the CLI option and the
  environment, at the moment of each request.
  Reading late matters: the providers register themselves, and create
  their sessions, when [[scholar.providers]] is imported---long before
  the CLI has parsed [[--proxy]].
\item[Application] Three ways for the settings to reach a request:
  [[get()]] for the one-off [[requests.get]] calls most providers make,
  [[ScholarSession]] (via [[new_session()]]) for code that keeps a
  session, and [[configure_pyalex()]] plus a session handed to the
  [[arxiv]] client for the two libraries that make requests for us.
\item[Reporting] [[describe_route()]] and [[exit_ip()]] for
  [[scholar providers check]].
\end{description}

[[get()]] is a thin wrapper that still calls [[requests.get]].
That keeps every existing test that patches [[requests.get]] working
unchanged, and it means a provider's request code changes by one name.

