\section{Applying the settings}

\subsection{Per-request options}

[[request_options]] is the one place the two settings meet a request.
It adds the User-Agent unless the caller set one---DBLP and arXiv
identify themselves on their own terms, and a caller that names itself
deliberately should be believed---and it adds the proxy as the
request's own [[proxies]] argument.

The per-request argument is not a matter of style.
[[requests]] lets the environment's proxy variables \emph{override} a
session's [[proxies]] attribute (the environment is merged into the
request before the session's defaults), so a session configured with
[[--proxy]] would quietly obey a stale [[HTTPS_PROXY]] instead.
Only a proxy passed with the request itself beats the environment.
When scholar has no proxy of its own we pass none, and the environment
applies exactly as it did before this module existed.

<<applying the settings>>=
def request_options(options: dict) -> dict:
    """Return request keyword arguments with scholar's identity and route.

    Adds the User-Agent unless `options` already sets one, and the
    configured proxy (if any) unless `options` already gives `proxies`.
    """
    options = dict(options)
    headers = dict(options.get("headers") or {})
    if not any(name.lower() == "user-agent" for name in headers):
        headers["User-Agent"] = user_agent()
    options["headers"] = headers
    proxy = proxy_url()
    if proxy and "proxies" not in options:
        options["proxies"] = {"http": proxy, "https": proxy}
    return options
@

\subsection{One-off requests}

<<applying the settings>>=
def get(url: str, **kwargs) -> requests.Response:
    """`requests.get` with scholar's User-Agent and proxy applied."""
    return requests.get(url, **request_options(kwargs))
@

\subsection{Sessions}

A session subclass applies the same options to every request it sends,
whichever method sends it.
Overriding [[request]] rather than [[get]] catches them all, since every
verb method of [[requests.Session]] delegates to it.
[[new_session]] adds the retry policy the callers mount today, for both
schemes, so moving a client onto it changes nothing else about how it
retries.

<<applying the settings>>=
class ScholarSession(requests.Session):
    """A `requests.Session` that applies scholar's identity and route."""

    def request(self, method, url, *args, **kwargs):
        return super().request(method, url, *args, **request_options(kwargs))


def new_session(retry: Retry | None = None) -> ScholarSession:
    """Return a `ScholarSession`, mounting `retry` for HTTP and HTTPS."""
    session = ScholarSession()
    if retry is not None:
        adapter = HTTPAdapter(max_retries=retry)
        session.mount("https://", adapter)
        session.mount("http://", adapter)
    return session
@

Let's verify that both paths carry the settings, that a caller's own
User-Agent survives, and---the property the per-request argument exists
for---that scholar's proxy beats a proxy in the environment.
The session test stops at [[send]], after [[requests]] has merged the
environment in, so it sees exactly what would go on the wire.

<<test functions>>=
class TestApplyingSettings:
    """Requests carry the identity and route."""

    def test_get_adds_user_agent_and_no_proxy_by_default(self):
        with patch("requests.get") as get_:
            network.get("https://example.org", params={"q": 1})
        kwargs = get_.call_args.kwargs
        assert kwargs["headers"]["User-Agent"] == user_agent()
        assert kwargs["params"] == {"q": 1}
        assert "proxies" not in kwargs

    def test_get_routes_through_the_proxy(self, monkeypatch):
        monkeypatch.setenv("SCHOLAR_PROXY", "http://proxy:3128")
        with patch("requests.get") as get_:
            network.get("https://example.org")
        assert get_.call_args.kwargs["proxies"] == {
            "http": "http://proxy:3128", "https": "http://proxy:3128",
        }

    def test_callers_user_agent_is_kept(self):
        options = request_options({"headers": {"user-agent": "arxiv.py"}})
        assert options["headers"] == {"user-agent": "arxiv.py"}

    def test_session_proxy_beats_the_environment(self, monkeypatch):
        monkeypatch.setenv("HTTPS_PROXY", "http://stale:9999")
        monkeypatch.setenv("SCHOLAR_PROXY", "http://proxy:3128")
        with patch.object(requests.Session, "send") as send:
            new_session().get("https://example.org")
        prepared = send.call_args.args[0]
        assert prepared.headers["User-Agent"] == user_agent()
        assert send.call_args.kwargs["proxies"]["https"] == (
            "http://proxy:3128"
        )

    def test_environment_proxy_applies_without_our_own(self, monkeypatch):
        monkeypatch.setenv("HTTPS_PROXY", "http://corporate:8080")
        with patch.object(requests.Session, "send") as send:
            new_session().get("https://example.org")
        assert send.call_args.kwargs["proxies"]["https"] == (
            "http://corporate:8080"
        )

    def test_new_session_mounts_the_retry_policy(self):
        retry = Retry(total=3)
        session = new_session(retry)
        adapter = session.get_adapter("https://example.org")
        assert adapter.max_retries.total == 3
