\section{Libraries that make their own requests}

Two providers talk to their APIs through client libraries, and each
needs the settings handed over in its own way.

The [[arxiv]] client keeps one [[requests.Session]] in its [[_session]]
attribute, so the arXiv provider simply gives it a [[ScholarSession]]
(\cref{sec:arxiv}).
The client sends its own User-Agent ([[arxiv.py/...]]) with each
request, which [[request_options]] respects; that name is honest and
identifies the library, and the route is what matters there.

[[pyalex]] is less accommodating.
It takes the User-Agent from its configuration, which we set, but it
creates a fresh session for every query through a module-level factory,
[[pyalex.api._get_requests_session]], and offers no proxy setting.
We wrap that factory: the wrapper lets [[pyalex]] build its session
(with its own retry policy), then moves the mounted adapters onto a
[[ScholarSession]].
The factory is private to [[pyalex]], so a release may rename it.
In that case the wrapper cannot be installed and OpenAlex would go
direct while everything else used the proxy; that is worth a warning,
and the warning is given only when a proxy is actually configured,
because without one nothing is lost.
The wrapper is installed once; [[OpenAlexProvider]] calls
[[configure_pyalex]] whenever it is constructed, which also refreshes the
User-Agent.
[[pyalex]] is imported inside the function, so that the modules needing
only [[get]] or [[new_session]] (Crossref, Unpaywall, the PDF module)
do not load it.

<<library configuration>>=
def configure_pyalex() -> None:
    """Hand scholar's identity and route to pyalex."""
    import pyalex
    import pyalex.api

    pyalex.config.user_agent = user_agent()
    factory = getattr(pyalex.api, "_get_requests_session", None)
    if factory is None:
        if proxy_url():
            logger.warning(
                "pyalex has no session factory to wrap; OpenAlex requests "
                "will not use the configured proxy"
            )
        return
    if getattr(factory, "_scholar_wrapped", False):
        return

    def scholar_session():
        session = new_session()
        for prefix, adapter in factory().adapters.items():
            session.mount(prefix, adapter)
        return session

    scholar_session._scholar_wrapped = True
    pyalex.api._get_requests_session = scholar_session
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_pyalex_sessions_carry_the_settings():
    """pyalex builds ScholarSessions with its own retry policy."""
    import pyalex
    import pyalex.api

    configure_pyalex()
    configure_pyalex()  # idempotent
    session = pyalex.api._get_requests_session()
    assert isinstance(session, ScholarSession)
    assert session.get_adapter("https://api.openalex.org").max_retries
    assert pyalex.config.user_agent == user_agent()
@


\section{Where the settings are applied}

The functions above only help where they are used.
Every client in scholar now goes through them: the providers' one-off
requests and health probes call [[get]]; Semantic Scholar, Crossref,
Unpaywall and the arXiv client keep [[ScholarSession]]s; OpenAlex and
its source-pinned siblings go through [[pyalex]], configured above; the
PDF module's resolvers and downloads call [[get]] (\cref{pdf-module}).
The one exception is DBLP, whose request loop is being rewritten
separately; until it moves over, DBLP keeps its own User-Agent and
follows only the environment's proxy variables.
The tests below pin the wiring down, so that a client that slips back
to a bare [[requests.Session]] or [[requests.get]] is noticed.

<<test functions>>=
