\section{Reporting the route}

[[scholar providers check]] states the route once, above its table.
Three answers are possible: scholar's own proxy (with its source),
a proxy [[requests]] will take from the environment, or none.
The second case is reported too, because it is the old implicit route,
and it is exactly where a forgotten variable---or a misspelt one, which
yields \enquote{direct}---shows up.
For the environment we ask [[requests]] itself, for the same probe URL a
provider would use, so [[NO_PROXY]] and the precedence among the
variables are applied by the code that will apply them to the requests.
A SOCKS proxy from the environment is not validated at start-up (it is
not ours), so the report says when PySocks is missing, since every
request would then fail.

<<route reporting>>=
def describe_route(url: str = ROUTE_PROBE_URL) -> str:
    """Describe how a request to `url` leaves this machine.

    Returns `"direct"` or `"proxy <scheme://host:port> (<source>)"`, with
    credentials removed.
    """
    configured = configured_proxy()
    if configured:
        proxy, source = configured
    else:
        environment = requests.utils.get_environ_proxies(url)
        proxy = requests.utils.select_proxy(url, environment)
        source = "environment"
    if not proxy:
        return "direct"
    description = f"proxy {redact_proxy(proxy)} ({source})"
    if proxy.lower().startswith("socks") and not socks_supported():
        description += " -- PySocks missing, SOCKS requests will fail"
    return description
<<constants>>=
ROUTE_PROBE_URL = "https://api.openalex.org"
@

The exit address answers the question the route cannot: did the tunnel
actually land on the campus network?
Finding it means asking a third party, which learns our address and
that we use scholar, so it happens only on request
([[providers check --show-ip]]), never by default.
The service is [[api.ipify.org]], which answers a plain GET with the
address as text.

<<route reporting>>=
def exit_ip() -> str:
    """Return this machine's public address as seen through the route.

    Contacts a third-party service; raises `requests.RequestException`
    on failure.
    """
    response = get(EXIT_IP_URL, timeout=EXIT_IP_TIMEOUT)
    response.raise_for_status()
    return response.text.strip()
<<constants>>=
EXIT_IP_URL = "https://api.ipify.org"
EXIT_IP_TIMEOUT = 10
@

